  "description": "A normalization of the project names the fixture fire-rating interpretation from Wednesday. Door 101 is a member through building A, and its fire-rating story ends on Wednesday; Door 120 sits in an annex outside the project, and its story is untouched. A read scoped to building A applies the project's normalization, even though the project is not in that scope: its frame resolves to the fire-rating interpretation and names the normalization. A read scoped to the annex resolves to literal comparison, and an explicit list naming both doors resolves to an ambiguous interpretation in force. When the annex is connected to the project on Friday, Door 120 comes under the normalization: the Connection write records its story's end act at Friday, cause normalization. When Door 101 is disconnected from building A on Saturday, it leaves the normalized scope and its sides differ literally again: the disconnect act's write records a related story whose beginning cites the normalization in force until then.",
  "note": "2.1.5: \"a read applies ... every normalization that applies to an in-scope Item, even when the scope Item it targets is not itself in the read's scope (7.3.8).\" 7.3.8: \"A normalization *applies to* an Item at an instant when the Item is a member of the normalization's scope at that instant (2.1.5, with the Connections active then)\"; T is \"its effective time, or a later instant at which the subject comes under it, by joining its scope\". 7.6.4: \"where none is in force from then because the subject left the normalized scope, the normalization in force until then.\" 7.2.8: maintenance applies to \"a Connection created, disconnected, reconnected or corrected that changes which normalizations apply to the subject\". README section 2: a created Connection that changes which normalizations apply \"also carries `generated` and `transition_acts`\"; section 5, Frame: for a list, \"ambiguous when they differ\", with `normalization` `{\"ambiguous\": [refs]}`.",
